ength left, she stayed in the comfort, the warmth that was enveloping her before her long sleep, she slightly felt the hands as they slipped under her, even heard the comforting voice and sudden warmth that accompanied the movement that was now taking place, but her eyes stayed shut, though rougher than she'd imagined, these must be the hands of god and she was being carried because floating didn't describe this but she was so tired, she herself to it and she drifted off……\nWhen she opened her eye's she immediately screwed them up and instantly shut them again, dazzled, blinded by the light. "I'm there" she thought to herself, though again, something wasn't right. She rustled with her hands and the rough hessian feel of a blanket clung there. This wasn't what she’d imagined, silk, something as smooth as silk was what she'd find in heaven then with a jolt she sat upright, and her eye's opened. If this wasn't heaven..........\nSomehow a sigh of relief came before she realised through the sheen of sweat now on her brow that this wasn't either, heaven or hell, this was still earthly, life. Her heart started to sink. She was still here. The hunger still there but, but, she was no longer cold, she felt the warmth from sunlight, she was inside, somewhere inside. No longer out there on hard cold stoney ground. She felt beneath her, what she sat on gave; it was soft, it was a mattress. The blanket though rough, was thick. Where was she......she started to panic.\nShe'd learned anything nice, that feeling of warmth was always followed by something cold hard and painful. She tensed at the memories from her past, this forced her to look again, with her eyes not her memory, that was too painful.\nShe was alone, in a small room, large grey stones like that of the church wall outside, she realised she must now be inside the church. The light was beating through a window above and behind her, the spectrum of colours visible and reflecting off the furniture, a table and chair. a rainbow in the room. There was heat coming from somewhere, she found the courage and leaned over the edge of the bed. An image of her as a child in the house she'd grown up in, skidded through in an instant, her as a girl, comfort she'd not appreciated at the age, but she'd not been aware of the horrors that were to befall her ever since. As she leaned over the edge from where the artificial heat that polluted the natural heat from the sunlight, she didn't care that it sort of didn't feel right, and she saw it, there a bar heater sat, 2 red orange glowing bars sat emanating a heat that didn't have the same feel as the suns that warmed rising against one side of her. It would do though and slipping her feet through the cold patch that existed where her feet hadn't been, and out into the air that wrapped itself around her skin, she brought the chilled feet to rest, toes wriggling, warming before the glowing bars. A smile washed across her face, it was a moment of respite, perhaps no more than that and she might have to pay later but for now, for that instant, she was going to feel that smile and enjoy that feeling that was for actually real.........\nAs she sat there, feet toasting, she heard an approaching set of footsteps. With military precision, the tock tock of heels on a stone floor echoed in the corridor outside, approaching. She felt herself stiffen, payment for the comfort was on its way she thought. As it got closer she heard something else with it, slightly out of time, but as she listened harder, she heard a sort of tock padf, padf, toc padf. It stopped as it reached the door. The door she was facing started opening and to her happy suprise, a gentle rounded almost comic face appeared with a smile round the door, bald, sparkling eyes and a bright white toothy smile. "Morning", the voice high pitched. There was a grunt behind him and the face lost it's smile as the body the face was attached to was pushed to one side and a thin tall woman with mouth stretched, showing a smile that was so false, forced. "So how is the patient this morning" she said and stopped dead. The smile draining when she saw the glare on the face coming back from the 'patient'. \n"What am i doing here, i never asked for this", she attacked, part of her defense. She wriggled her toes, expressing her courage and freedom, even more in defiance at what she expected would be unfriendliness from the woman now. The man came out from behind the door. He was short as well and dressed like a monk. Dorothy couldn’t hold the shock as she was amazed, people didn't dress like that any more she thought. If it wasn't for the fact that the woman was wearing jeans and for the amount of makeup plastered on her face, she'd  have sworn she was dreaming and this was the afterlife. Her swinging toes touched the heater in her exuberance to show how much she didn't care. She jumped back automatically suddenly aware of the danger. The thin woman smirked for an instant but it was long enough for Dorothy to pick up on it. She heard the door creak as another person arrived. Dorothy then met the priest, the man who ran this rural church. His smile caught her first, he was blond, eye’s were blue as sky and teeth that shone. She noticed visibly the softening of both of the early entrants into her room, eyes lowering in deference as he humbly yet confidently walked in. Long black gown and white collar suited him, she could almost imagine a halo. She pinched herself. He audibly whispered to her “Dorothy, nice name. You were in a fever when,” and he turned to the tall woman “Andrea brought you in. I think you should thank her, she might have saved your life, you were frozen child, when did you last eat?”.\n\nlater in the afternoon, she’d learned the names of the Tall woman and short fat man, they weren’t as bad as she’d originally thought, there had been no punishment, no whipping or worse as she’d expected, it had actually been quite pleasant and the week had continued though she’d 